=\"e2\">\nLarge “nexelia” logo in blue with a purple stylized “x” and a blue dot above the final “i”.\n</visual_element>\n\nAssociated with : 2014/10487  \nRegistration of this trade mark shall give no right to the exclusive use of the word \"TECHNOLOGIES\", separately and apart from the mark.  \nFILED: 2014-07-28\n\n2014/19674 in Class 01: Adhesives sold in ready to use cartridges for industrial use in underground mines and underground construction. in the name of ROCBOLT TECHNOLOGIES (PROPRIETARY) LIMITED, a South African company 30 North Reef Road Elandsfontein Germiston 1412, South Africa. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCERTIFICATE OF REGISTRATION (https://www.rocboltresins.com.au/_files/ugd/768559_e8311a8b2dcf41c08e9cf4636246d2cb.pdf)\nciteturn111search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; CERTIFICATE OF REGISTRATION ... **Rocbolt Resins Pty Ltd**\nCERTIFICATE OF REGISTRATION\n\nIntertek SAI GLOBAL\n\nThis is to certify that:  \n**Rocbolt Resins Pty Ltd**  \nABN 70 123 943 402\n\n40-44 Anzac Avenue Smeaton Grange NSW 2567 AUSTRALIA\n\noperates a\n\n**QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM**\n\nwhich complies with the requirements of\n\n**ISO 9001:2015**\n\nfor the following scope\n\nThe manufacture of ground support resin capsules for the mining and civil industry.\n\nCertificate Number:  \nQMS40664\n\nOriginally Certified:  \n21 July 2014\n\nCurrent Certification:  \n7 April 2023\n\nIssue Date:  \n11 April 2023\n\nExpiry Date:  \n20 July 2026\n\nCertified System  \nISO 9001\n\nJAS-ANZ  \nWWW.JAS-ANZ.ORG/REGISTER\n\nCalin Moldovean  \nPresident, Business Assurance\n\nSAI Global Certification Services Pty. Ltd.  \n650 Lorimer Street  \nPort Melbourne Victoria 3207  \nAustralia\n\nRegistered by:  \nSAI Global Certification Services Pty Ltd (ACN 108 716 669) 650 Lorimer Street Port Melbourne VIC 3207 Australia with SAI Global Pty Limited 650 Lorimer Street Port Melbourne VIC 3207 Australia (“SAI Global”) and subject to the SAI Global Terms and Conditions for Certification. While all due care and skill was exercised in carrying out this assessment, SAI Global accepts responsibility only for proven negligence. This certificate remains the property of SAI Global and must be returned to SAI Global upon its request. To verify that this certificate is current please refer to SAI Global On-Line Certification register at http://register.saiglobal.com/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRocbolt Technologies (Pty) Ltd v Rockbolt (Pty) Ltd and Another (CT00600ADJ2021) [2021] COMPTRI 23 (19 April 2021) (https://www3.saflii.org/za/cases/COMPTRI/2021/23.html)\nciteturn111search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; 2.1     that the First Respondent change its name ROCKBOLT to one which does not incorporate the word ROCBOLT or any other word confusingly and or deceptively similar to its ROCBOLT trademarks in terms of Section 160 of the Act, ... (iii) a registered trade mark belonging to a person other than the company, or a mark in respect of which an application has been filed in the Republic for registration as a trade mark or a well-known trade mark as contemplated in section 35 of the Trade Marks Act, 1993 (Act No. 194 of 1993); or (iv) a mark, word or expression the use of which is restricted or protected in terms of the Merchandise Marks Act, 1941 (Act No. 17 of 1941), except to the extent permitted by or in terms of that Act;\n\n \n\n \n\nPresiding Member of the Companies Tribunal: Lucia Glass\n\nDECISION (Reasons and Order)\n\n1.  The Applicant applies for a default order against the First Respondent, in respect of its company name, ROCKBOLT, which is confusingly similar to the Applicant’s ROCKBOLT trademarks, which offends against the provisions of Section 11(2) of the Companies Act 71 of 2008 (further referred to as “the Act”)\n\n2.  The Applicant applies for the following orders;\n\n \n\n2.1     that the First Respondent change its name ROCKBOLT to one which does not incorporate the word ROCBOLT or any other word confusingly and or deceptively similar to its ROCBOLT trademarks in terms of Section 160 of the Act,\n\n \n\n2.2    that the Registrar of Companies be directed to change the name of the First Respondent to its registration number, in the event of the First Respondent does not comply with paragraph 3 above, within 60 days of the order,\n\n \n\n2.3  further that the First Respondent effect payment of the costs of this matter in terms of Regulation 156 of the Act.\n\n \n\n3.  The Respondent Company was registered in 2019 and the Applicant’s Trademarks were registered in and around 2014. Thus the First Respondent Company, was registered almost 5 years after the Applicant’s Trademarks were registered. Thus the Applicant’s trademarks were well established when the first Respondent’s name was registered.\n\n \n\n4.   A proper Service of this Application was served on the Director of the First Respondent, Mr Pule Hendrik Mfalwane, at 1[...] M[...] Street, Letabong, on the 12 th February 2021, by the Sheriff.\n\n \n\n5.  The First Respondent has to date not entered a responding affidavit. Subsequently I am satisfied that this Application was properly served on the First Respondent, and to date, the First Respondent has not deemed it necessary to defend this Application.\n\n \n\n6.  The Deponent to the Applicant's founding affidavit, is Hendrik Petrus Groenewald, who avers that he is the Applicant Company’s Secretary and Financial manager and attaches a Resolution authorising him to act on behalf of the Applicant, in these proceedings\n\n \n\n7.  It is averred that the First Respondent’s name ROCKBOLT, is confusingly similar to the Applicant’s ROCBOLT trademarks and would reasonably mislead a person to incorrectly believe that the Respondent is part of or associated with the Applicant. Further that the First Respondent’s name ROCKBOLT is the dominant and memorable element of the name which is phonetically and conceptually identical to the Applicant’s ROCBOLT trademarks.\n\nIt is however, also obvious that there are similarities. The name Kintronics incorporates the whole of the applicants trading style Kentron.\"\n\n \n\n13.  In 2001 the court held:[3] \"the decision involves a value judgment and that the ultimate test is whether, on a comparison of the two marks it can properly be said that there is a reasonable likelihood of confusion if both marks are to be used together in a normal and fair manner, in the ordinary course of business\".\n\n \n\nREASONS AND DECISION\n\n \n\n14.  The Applicant’s trademarks, contain the word ROCBOLT, which is spelt almost the same way as First Respondent’s Name ROCKBOLT. Phonetically the names are identical. If the Applicant’s trademarks and the First Respondent’s name are compared, there is no doubt in my mind that they are confusingly similar, as they both have a phonetically identical word, which will more than likely lead to confusion.\n\n \n\n15.  If the name ROCKBOLT, is to be used by the First Respondent, and ROCBOLT, is to be used by the Applicant, in a normal and fair manner and in an ordinary course business, i t is my view, that if members of the public merely look at the names of the different entities, they will be misled by the similarity of the names.\n\n \n\n16.   Members of the public will be confused or deceived into believing that the business of the Applicant is linked to, or, associated with that of the First Respondent as the name of the First Respondent falsely implies and suggests, and is such, that it would reasonably mislead a person to believe incorrectly, that the First Respondent company is part of, or associated with the Applicant’s trademarks. (emphasis added)\n\n# ORDER\n\n \n\nI proceed to make the following Order:\n\n \n\na)  The First Respondent is directed to change its name ROCKBOLT to one which does not incorporate the word ROCKBOLT or any other word confusingly and or deceptively similar to Applicant’s ROCBOLT trademarks.\n\n \n\nb)  The Registrar of Companies is directed to change the name of the First Respondent to its registration number, in the event of the First Respondent not complying with paragraph (a) above, within 3 months of the order.\n\n \n\nc)  The First Respondent is exempted from the requirement, to pay the prescribed fee for filing the notice of amendment contemplated in this paragraph.\n\n \n\nd)  T his determination is to be served on the 1 st and 2 nd Respondents.\n\n \n\ne)   Any other person with an interest in the name that is the subject of this Application, may, within twenty (20) business days after receiving the notice of this determination and administrative order, apply to a court to review the determination.\n\n \n\nf)     No order as to costs.\n\n \n\nLUCIA GLASS\n\n \n\n(MEMBER OF COMPANIES TRIBUNAL OF SOUTH AFRICA)\n\n \n\nDated this 19th April 2021\n\n[1] AMERICAN CHEWING PRODUCTS CORPORATION v AMERICAN CHICLE COMPANY 1948 (2) SA 736 (A)\n\n[2] DENEL (PTY) LTD AND KENTRONICS (PTY) LTD AND THE REGISTRAR OF COMPANIES TDP CASE NO 213527/2000 (unreported)\n\n[3] COWBELL AG V ICS HOLDINGS 2001 (3) SA 941 (SCA)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRocbolt Technologies (pty) Ltd to Johannesburg, South Africa | Kompass (https://xk.kompass.com/c/rocbolt-technologies-pty-ltd/zan608806/)\nciteturn111search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Rocbolt Technologies (Pty) Ltd ( Johannesburg) ... Registration No.\n\n# Rocbolt Technologies (Pty) Ltd ( Johannesburg)\n\nJohannesburg 1619- South Africa\n\n## Presentation\n\nFibreglass & Resins Suppliers\n\nRead more Collapse\n\n## Informations\n\n### General\n\nDiscover more on our Website  | http://www.rocbolt.com\nKompass ID^{?}  | ZAN608806\nYear established  | \nNo employees (address)  | 50-99 Employees\nNo employees  | 50-99 Employees\n\n### Legal\n\nType  | Headquarters\nYear established  | \nActivity